{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788178234,"nonce":"pHdXnLJbZfo5M6weFT0cHX4gg5Q=","message":"WORKER REPORT — spec 3.2 built on branch `spec/3.2-designate-zones`, BORGES,\n2026-08-31. Five commits, no DLL, `dotnet build -c Release` clean (0 warnings,\n0 errors). The acceptance script is committed at `accept/3.2-designate-zones.md`.\n\nOpen-question resolutions, each with its rationale.\n\nOQ1 — FILTER SPEC FORMAT (tree vs presets+patches). PRESETS ONLY, and the\nvocabulary is closed at five words plus two. `zone add`/`zone edit` take\n`filter: \"meds\"` or `filter: [\"food\",\"meds\"]` from the set\nfood | meds | apparel | weapons | raw | all | none, applied as a whole-filter\nREPLACEMENT: `ThingFilter.SetDisallowAll()` then one\n`SetAllow(ThingCategoryDef, true)` per word — the Clear-All button\n(`Verse/ThingFilterUI.DoThingFilterConfigWindow`) followed by the category\ncheckbox (`Verse/Listing_TreeThingFilter.DoCategoryChildren`). Passing an\nOBJECT to `filter` is refused with a bad-args naming spec 3.6 and\n`IStoreSettingsParent`.\n\nWhy not a tree, and where the seam is: the session-4 amendment moved storage\nsettings to 3.6 because `Zone_Stockpile` and `Building_Storage` share one API,\nso a tree format invented here would be re-invented there and the two would\ndiverge. Replacement rather than patch is what makes a fixture reproducible —\nthe same call twice leaves the same filter. So 3.6 inherits a clean line:\n**anything finer than the five category presets is 3.6's** — def-level\nallow/disallow, hit-point and quality ranges, special filters, per-node\npatches, copy/paste between storages, storage GROUPS — for stockpile zones and\nstorage buildings alike. Nothing in 3.2 touches a `ThingFilter` except through\n`ApplyPreset`, which is fifteen lines in `ZoneVerbs.cs`.\n\nOQ2 — RECT VS FREE-CELL SYNTAX. FOUR MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE KEYS, one shape each,\nnever overloaded: `rect: [x,z,w,h]` · `cells: [P, ...]` (P is any\n`Positions.Resolve` form, so landmarks / `pawn:\u003cid\u003e` / `thing:\u003cid\u003e` work) ·\n`things: [id, ...]` · `filter: {def|category, rect, forbidden, faction, max}`\n(the spec's `--area-things`). Every verb in the spec takes the same four.\n\nThe reason it is not one `cells` key that accepts both: a four-element array is\ngenuinely ambiguous between a rect and four positions, and the caller is a\nprogram. Two keys cost one line of documentation; a wrong guess costs a\nsilently mis-shaped zone. `max_cells` caps a walk (default 2500, ceiling\n20000) and `capped`/`requested` report what was dropped — the truncation\ncontract the rest of the mod already follows.\n\nOQ3 — HOW `cancel` SCOPES. TO WHAT IT IS POINTED AT, AND NOTHING ELSE. `cancel`\nis `Designator_Cancel` driven over the same four target forms: every cancelable\ndesignation on the targeted cells or things, plus player blueprints and frames,\nwhich is exactly that designator's own behaviour. There is deliberately no\n\"cancel all designations of type X map-wide\". The game's version of that is a\nright-click float-menu option on an already-selected designator\n(`Designator.RightClickFloatMenuOptions`, \"RemoveAllDesignations\"), i.e. two\ndeliberate clicks, and a verb that clears the whole map by default is precisely\nthe god-hand this spec exists to avoid. An agent that wants it can pass the\nrect.\n\nResolutions BEYOND the three open questions, because each changed what got\nbuilt.\n\nR1 — `chop` IS `Designator_PlantsHarvestWood`, and the scope list's missing verb\nis `cut`. The scope names `chop` and `harvest-wood` as separate entries, but the\nEnglish label of `Designator_PlantsHarvestWood` is literally \"Chop wood\"\n(`Core/Languages/English/Keyed`, `DesignatorHarvestWood`), so they are one\ndesignator under two names and both are registered. `Designator_PlantsCut`\n(\"Cut plants\") had no name in the scope at all; it is `cut` (alias\n`cut-plants`). This matters for acceptance bullet 1: `Designator_PlantsCut\n.AffectsThing` refuses a HARVESTABLE tree, so a `chop` that meant PlantsCut\nwould reject every mature tree in the rect.\n\nR2 — `isOrder = true` ON EVERY DESIGNATOR WE BUILD.\n`Verse/DesignationCategoryDef.ResolveDesignators` sets it on every designator\nbuilt from `specialDesignatorClasses`, i.e. on every architect-menu designator;\n`ReverseDesignatorDatabase` does not. It is not cosmetic — it is the only thing\nseparating `Designator_PlantsCut.AffectsThing`'s two behaviours. `designate` is\na drag over a rect, so it is the architect surface.\n\nR3 — FOG IS GATED BY US, UNIFORMLY, BEFORE THE DESIGNATOR. Not a style choice:\nthe game's own designators disagree with each other, and one is actively wrong\nfor us. `Designator_Mine.CanDesignateCell` RETURNS TRUE for a fogged cell (the\nplayer may paint mining over unexplored rock); `Designator_Haul`,\n`Designator_Claim`, `Designator_ZoneAdd.IsZoneableCell` and `Designator_Smooth`\nrefuse; `Designator_Deconstruct` and `Designator_Uninstall` refuse unless\ngodMode; `Designator_AreaAllowedExpand` does not check at all. DESIGN's\ndecisions log makes it one rule for the whole player-facing surface, so the\nengine rejects a fogged target with `why:\"fogged\"`, `reason:\"unexplored\"`,\n`removal:\"none\"` and the designator never sees it. Acceptance step E6 is the\ndirect evidence. The one exception, named in the code: `Area.Invert` flips\nevery cell on the map including unexplored ground, because the game's own\nbutton does — `area allowed invert` says so in its result.\n\nR4 — `Designator.Finalize` IS CALLED ON SUCCESS, NEVER ON FAILURE.\n`Finalize(false)` is pure UI (a sound plus `Find.DesignatorManager.Dragger\n.FailureReason` posted as a Message). `Finalize(true)` is NOT:\n`Designator_AreaNoRoof.FinalizeDesignationSucceeded` clears `BuildRoof` for the\ncells it just added AND drains a STATIC `justAddedCells` list, so skipping it\nwould both lose the model change and leak our cells into the next real player\ndrag. It is also where Hunt/Slaughter/Tame's own warnings come from, and those\nare journaled `message` events the agent should have. `DesignateMultiCell` is\nnever called — it re-enters TutorSystem and, for zones, drives `Find.Selector`.\n\nR5 — `zone add growing` REFUSES WITHOUT `plant` (opt out with\n`allow_unset_plant:true`). The session-4 amendment said \"refuse or warn\"; a\nwarning in a result field is a warning nothing reads, and the doctrine is\nsharp — a growing zone with no plant grows the game's default, which is why the\nscripted tutorial names rice explicitly.\n\nR6 — `zone edit {hidden}` IS NOT IMPLEMENTED, deliberately. The game has the\ntoggle (`Command_Hide_ZoneStockpile`), but its setter iterates `Zone.Cells` —\nthe Fisher-Yates getter that permanently reorders a SCRIBED list and advances\nthe shared Rand stream (WorldSafe Class R). It is a rendering toggle no work\ngiver reads, so the agent gains nothing and the save pays for it.\n\nR7 — FISHING ZONES ARE OUT. The amendment lists `ZoneAdd_Fishing`, but\n`Designator_ZoneAdd_Fishing.Visible` reads `ResearchProjectDefOf.Fishing\n.IsFinished`, which bottoms out in `ResearchManager.GetProgress` and INSERTS a\nzero entry into a scribed dictionary (WorldSafe Class A). Merely offering the\ntype would make `designate`'s generic `Visible` gate write the save. Fishing\nneeds `WorldSafe`-guarded research reads first; filing it rather than shipping\na write.\n\nR8 — `RemovePaint` / `RemoveFloorPaint` ARE OUT, and this is a spec conflict\nworth recording: the session-4 amendment lists `RemovePaint` among the missing\ndesignators, while DESIGN's non-goals say \"Cosmetics (the `Designator_Paint`\nfamily, styling stations, glower colour) ... are out of scope. Named here so\nnobody specs them later.\" DESIGN wins; the amendment's bullet is stale.\n\nR9 — THE `flick` VERB DROPS EXACTLY ONE LINE OF\n`FlickUtility.UpdateFlickDesignation`, AND IT IS A HAZARD, NOT A SHORTCUT. That\nmethod ends with `TutorUtility.DoModalDialogIfNotKnown(ConceptDefOf\n.SwitchFlickingDesignation)`, which on a save that has never flicked anything\nruns `Find.WindowStack.Add(new Dialog_MessageBox(msg))` — and\n`Verse/Dialog_MessageBox` sets `forcePause = true`. Per JOURNAL.md (spec 1.7) a\nforce-pausing window halts EVERY subsequent `advance` with `reason:\"dialog\"`,\nis not suppressible, and cannot be closed from here. So the designation half is\nreproduced line for line and the tutorial modal is dropped. Nothing about the\nmodel differs. Acceptance step F3 is the check. The same trap sits in\n`Command_SetPlantToGrow`'s `NoGrowerCanPlant` path, which is why `zone add\ngrowing` reports that condition as a `warnings` string instead of reproducing\nthe dialog.\n\nR10 — `area allowed assign pawn` IS NOT IN 3.2. Per the orchestrator's\nmid-build cross-spec ruling: `Pawn_PlayerSettings\n.AreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap` is a pawn field driven by\n`PawnColumnWorker_AllowedArea` in the Assign tab, and it is 3.4's. This spec's\nscope line (\"area allowed create/name/assign pawn\") is therefore split and its\nlast third is now 3.4's. Nothing here assigns a pawn, and the acceptance script\nsays so rather than closing the loop another way.\n\nR11 — `designate claim` IS THE ONE TYPE THAT IS NOT A DESIGNATION.\n`Designator_Claim.DesignateThing` calls `t.SetFaction(Faction.OfPlayer)` on the\nspot; no work order, no colonist walks anywhere. The result carries a `note`\nsaying so and `designations_before`/`designations_now` are `null` rather than\n0, because \"there is no such count\" and \"the count is zero\" must not read\nalike. Same for `smooth`, which picks wall or floor per cell and so has no\nsingle designation def.","files":null}]}